<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · War and march · 1792 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">La Marseillaise</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A marching song written in one night in 1792 in Strasbourg that became the national anthem of France.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Claude Joseph Rouget de Lisle</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Claude Joseph Rouget de Lisle</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>French revolutionaries; later the French Republic</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Strasbourg, France</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>25 April 1792</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem · War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Claude Joseph Rouget de Lisle (1792; he died in 1836))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The monarchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> feared that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> would spread, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-first-coalition">War of the First Coalition</a> they tried to stop it. The Kingdom of the French declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, the French army fared badly at first, and Coalition armies invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. On 25 April 1792 Baron Philippe Friedrich Dietrich, mayor of Strasbourg, asked his guest Claude Joseph Rouget de Lisle to write a song to rally soldiers to defend the threatened country. That evening Rouget de Lisle wrote &quot;Chant de guerre pour l'Armée du Rhin&quot; (&quot;War <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> for the Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>&quot;) and dedicated it to Marshal Nicolas Luckner. A plaque on Place Broglie marks the site of Dietrich's house. Dietrich himself was executed the next year, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reign-of-terror">Reign of Terror</a>.</p><p>The song took its lasting name from volunteers (fédérés) from Marseille. A young volunteer from Montpellier, François Mireur, sang it at a patriotic gathering in Marseille, the city's National Guard adopted it as its marching song, and the fédérés sang it in the streets as they marched to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, which they entered on 30 July 1792. It spread quickly as a song of the Revolution. Because most Alsatians did not speak French, a German version was published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colmar">Colmar</a> in October 1792. The invading armies were driven back after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-valmy">Battle of Valmy</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> made it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s first national anthem by a decree of 14 July 1795. It lost that status under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> I, was banned outright by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xviii">Louis XVIII</a> and Charles X, and returned only briefly after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/july-revolution">July Revolution</a> of 1830. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>'s government brought it back to rally the country in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a> of 1871 sang it with new words. In 1879 it was restored as the national anthem and has remained so since.</p><p>Through the 19th and early 20th centuries it was the anthem of revolutionary movements well beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, Peter Lavrov wrote new Russian words to the melody in 1875; this &quot;Worker's Marseillaise&quot; was sung in the Revolution of 1905 and served as the semi-official anthem of the Russian republic after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a> of 1917. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> it was used as a protest song in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-first-movement">March First Movement</a> against Japanese rule in 1919. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-schumann">Robert Schumann</a> quoted it in 1839, singing it was forbidden as a piece that might cause public disorder.</p><p>Its words have long drawn criticism. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeremy-bentham">Jeremy Bentham</a> called it &quot;the war whoop of anarchy&quot; in 1796. In the 1970s President Valéry Giscard d'Estaing objected to singing its violent lines against Prussian enemies while a German chancellor stood beside him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and a 1992 campaign by more than 100 prominent French citizens, among them Danielle Mitterrand, failed to change them. Serge Gainsbourg's reggae version of 1979 was attacked in Le Figaro. Usually only the first verse is sung today.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A call to arms: citizens are summoned to take up weapons and march against invading armies and the tyranny they bring, to protect their homes and families. The mood is urgent and fierce, with vivid images of bloodshed, and it ends in a refrain that commands the people to form their battalions.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">French</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">French</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Allons, enfans de la Patrie !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us go, children of the Fatherland!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Le jour de gloire est arrivé.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The day of glory has arrived.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Contre nous de la tyrannie</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Against us, of tyranny</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">L’étendard sanglant est levé.(bis)</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The bloody standard is raised. (bis)</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Entendez-vous dans les campagnes</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Do you hear in the countryside</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mugir ces féroces soldats ?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Those ferocious soldiers bellowing?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ils viennent jusque dans nos bras,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>They come right into our arms</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Égorger vos fils, vos compagnes !…</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To cut the throats of your sons, your companions!…</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Aux armes, citoyens ! formez vos bataillons.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To arms, citizens! form your battalions.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Marchons (bis), qu’un sang impur abreuve nos sillons !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us march (bis), may an impure blood water our furrows!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Que veut cette horde d’esclaves</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What does this horde of slaves want,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">De traîtres, de rois conjurés ?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Of traitors, of conspiring kings?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pour qui ces ignobles entraves,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For whom these base shackles,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ces fers dès longtemps préparés ?(bis)</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>These irons long since prepared? (bis)</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Français ! pour nous, ah ! quel outrage !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Frenchmen! for us, ah! what an outrage!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Quels transports il doit exciter ?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What passions it must arouse!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">C’est nous qu’on ose méditer</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It is we whom they dare to plan</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">De rendre à l’antique esclavage…</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To return to the ancient slavery…</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Aux armes, citoyens ! etc.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To arms, citizens! etc.</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 56 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Quoi des cohortes étrangères</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What! foreign cohorts</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Feraient la loi dans nos foyers !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Would make the law in our homes!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Quoi ces phalanges mercenaires</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What! these mercenary phalanxes</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Terrasseraient nos fiers guerriers !(bis)</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Would strike down our proud warriors! (bis)</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Grand Dieu !.. Par des mains enchainées.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Great God!.. By chained hands</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Nos fronts sous le joug ploiraient ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Our brows would bend beneath the yoke;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">De vils despotes deviendraient</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Vile despots would become</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Les maîtres de nos destinées !…</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The masters of our destinies!…</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Aux armes, citoyens ! etc.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To arms, citizens! etc.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Tremble, tyrans ! et vous, perfides.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Tremble, tyrants! and you, treacherous ones,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">L’opprobre de tous les partis.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The disgrace of all parties.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Tremblez !… Vos projets parricides</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Tremble!… Your parricidal schemes</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Vont enfin recevoir leur prix. (bis)</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Are at last going to receive their due. (bis)</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Tout est soldat pour vous combattre.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Everyone is a soldier to fight you.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">S’ils tombent nos jeunes héros,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>If they fall, our young heroes,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">La terre en produit de nouveaux</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The earth produces new ones</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Contre vous tous prêts à se battre</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Against you, all ready to fight</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Aux armes, citoyens ! etc.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To arms, citizens! etc.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Français ! en guerriers magnanimes</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Frenchmen! as magnanimous warriors</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Portez ou retenez vos coups.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Strike or hold back your blows.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Épargnez ces tristes victimes</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Spare these sorry victims</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">À regret s’armant contre nous. (bis)</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Arming themselves against us with regret. (bis)</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mais le despote sanguinaire !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But the bloodthirsty despot!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mais les complices de Bouillé !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But the accomplices of Bouillé!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Tous ces tigres qui sans pitié</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>All these tigers who without pity</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Déchirent le sein de leur mère.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Tear the breast of their mother.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Aux armes, citoyens ! forme ; vos bataillons.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To arms, citizens! form your battalions.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Marchons (bis) ; que tout leur sang abreuve nos sillons !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us march (bis); may all their blood water our furrows!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Amour sacré de la Patrie,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Sacred love of the Fatherland,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Conduis, soutiens nos bras vengeurs :</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Lead, support our avenging arms:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Liberté ! liberté chérie</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Liberty! cherished liberty</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Combats avec tes défenseurs. (bis)</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Fight with your defenders. (bis)</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Sous nos drapeaux que la Victoire</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Under our flags may Victory</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Accoure à tes mâles accents :</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hasten at your manly strains:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Que tes ennemis expirants</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>May your dying enemies</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Voient ton triomphe et notre gloire.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>See your triumph and our glory.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Aux armes citoyens !…</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To arms, citizens!…</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Claude Joseph Rouget de Lisle (1792); the text of 1792. Text from <a href="https://fr.wikisource.org/wiki/La_Marseillaise_(1792)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (12)</summary><ul><li>General: this is the 1792 text of six stanzas; the 'children's stanza' ('Nous entrerons dans la carrière') was added later and is not in it. 'enfans' is the period spelling of 'enfants'. '(bis)' and 'etc.' are the source's repeat signs, kept as they stand.</li><li>line 1.10: 'qu'un sang impur abreuve nos sillons' — 'impure blood' is read by most historians as the enemies' (the invaders'); its exact sense is disputed and has been argued over since. Translated word for word.</li><li>lines 1.3-1.4: the word order of the original is kept ('Against us, of tyranny / the bloody standard is raised' = the bloody standard of tyranny is raised against us).</li><li>line 1.8: 'compagnes' are female companions, i.e. wives.</li><li>line 2.6: printed with a question mark in the source; 'transports' here means outbursts of feeling (fury, passion). Rendered as an exclamation.</li><li>lines 2.7-2.8: one sentence across two lines: 'it is we whom they dare to plan to return to the ancient slavery'.</li><li>lines 3.5-3.6: one sentence: 'by chained hands our brows would bend beneath the yoke'; the source's full stop after 'enchainées' is not followed.</li><li>line 4.1: the source prints 'Tremble' (not 'Tremblez') though tyrants are addressed in the plural.</li><li>line 5.2: Bouillé: François Claude, Marquis de Bouillé, the general who crushed the Nancy mutiny of 1790 and organised the king's flight to Varennes in 1791.</li><li>line 5.8: 'their mother' is the Fatherland, France.</li><li>line 5.9: the source prints 'forme ;' — a misprint for 'formez', translated as such.</li><li>line 6.6: 'tes mâles accents' — the manly (virile) voice or strains of Liberty.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="JpbDZkI0rnA" data-title="La Marseillaise: The Chœur de l'Armée française"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">The Chœur de l'Armée française</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Chœur de l'Armée française - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JpbDZkI0rnA" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Auf, Brüder, auf dem Tag entgegen</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1792</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation (German, published in Colmar)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">La Marseillaise de la Commune</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1871</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words, same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Worker's Marseillaise (Peter Lavrov)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1875</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new Russian words, same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Arrangement for soprano, chorus and orchestra (Hector Berlioz)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">c. 1830</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Piano transcription (Franz Liszt)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">transcription</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Faschingsschwank aus Wien (Robert Schumann)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1839</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Die beiden Grenadiere (Robert Schumann)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1840</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Les deux grenadiers (Richard Wagner)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1840</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Battle of Manassas (Thomas Wiggins)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">c. 1861</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">1812 Overture (Tchaikovsky)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1880</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Maria Antonietta (Pavlos Carrer)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1884</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Marcha Filipina-Magdalo, later Lupang Hinirang (Julián Felipe)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1898</span><span class="sg-chain-h">elements incorporated</span></li><li><a href="/songs/canto-degli-italiani">Il Canto degli italiani</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1847</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source of inspiration for the text</span></li><li><a href="/songs/lupang-hinirang">Lupang Hinirang (Chosen Land)</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1898</span><span class="sg-chain-h">one of three pieces Felipe said he based the music on</span></li><li><a href="/songs/thai-national-anthem">Thai National Anthem</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1932</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the model asked for by Luang Nithetkolakit</span></li><li><a href="/songs/the-internationale">The Internationale</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1871</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Pottier wrote the words to be sung to its tune</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Piano Concerto No. 25, Allegro maestoso (Mozart)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1786</span><span class="sg-chain-h">cited as a possible antecedent of the melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Esther, oratorio (Jean Baptiste Lucien Grison)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1787</span><span class="sg-chain-h">cited as a possible antecedent of the melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Tema e variazioni in Do maggiore (Giovanni Battista Viotti)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1795</span><span class="sg-chain-h">cited as an antecedent, but apparently published after the anthem</span></li></ul></section>
